{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792955552,"nonce":"wCo5kQcVXeWa4y/QKuLRjQz6qj8=","message":"Completed on branch explicit-target-generic-cpu in 83e2f7750c4cd9e7f93fe419a43f60bdedc8eb44. Scope confirmed by the owner: finish the generic-CPU fix AND implement foreign OS/architecture executable linking.\n\nAn explicit --target now selects a generic CPU, including the host's triple; an implicit target retains host tuning. The policy is scoped to the AOT request and restored on errors. Optimizer machine caches separate CPU policy and feature settings. Native ABI matching handles arm64/aarch64 and Darwin version spelling, so the macos-aarch64 release alias can use the host runtime rather than being mistaken for a foreign architecture. Release aliases and Rust's riscv64gc/riscv32gc spellings are normalized.\n\nNative cross links use target runtime names, target system libraries, loader paths and staged runtime names. Runtime lookup uses target subdirectories or explicit overrides; foreign builds never fall back to the host's embedded runtime. A target GCC driver is checked with -dumpmachine; Clang/Clang-CL get the target and LLD selection; configured Zig gets its cc target. Target-specific ASH_LINKER, CARGO_TARGET_*_LINKER, CC, ASH_SYSROOT, ASH_RUNTIME and ASH_LINK_ARGS are supported, with quoted driver/extra arguments passed without a shell. docs/cli.md contains setup and invocation examples. Matching SDK/libc, linker and runtime must be installed; the change does not bundle commercial SDKs or every target runtime.\n\nValidation:\n- 7 target ABI/CPU tests and 3 native runtime/linker tests passed, including CPU-policy restoration and cache separation.\n- Named native builds and the native release alias both link and execute with cpu generic and exact interpreter output.\n- Existing 8-target ELF emission gate passed. Distribution tests additionally validate x86_64/aarch64 Mach-O and COFF plus the Rust RISC-V spelling.\n- cargo clippy -p ash_core --lib -- -D warnings, cargo clippy -p ash --tests -- -D warnings, and a no-default-features ash_core check passed. Workflow YAML and git diff --check passed.\n- Actual cross-executable gate: the final compiler on this arm64 Mac generated and linked x86_64 Linux binaries for test_basic.hl and test_stdlib.hl. All four NUC executions (both fixtures with static and staged shared runtimes) matched the original interpreter stdout exactly. The shared variants add an unused OPTIONAL HDLL declaration to select genuine shared-runtime code generation through --build. Test result: configured_cross_executable_matches_the_reference passed in 52.34 seconds.\n\nLocal test resources were isolated under /tmp/ash-cross-linux and /tmp/ash-cross-bin, using the NUC's Oct-24 /home/raikage/ash-b/target/release runtimes and a copied Linux link sysroot. The installed Homebrew LLD 20/LLVM 21 pair could not start, so the test driver used Rust's self-contained LLD via a local wrapper. NUC execution used a fresh temporary directory per program and removed it afterwards. Existing NUC checkouts were not changed.\n\nCI coverage added: aot_distribution joins the native AOT matrix (Linux x86_64/arm64, macOS, Windows), and a new Ubuntu job cross-builds the ARM64 runtime, links both static and shared fixture variants, and executes them under qemu-aarch64. These new CI jobs have not been dispatched/run by this checkpoint; the local checks and actual Mac-to-NUC execution above are the completed evidence. Windows/macOS foreign SDK execution is not claimed.\n\nThe unrelated pre-existing test_simd.hl edit was excluded. The implementation is committed on its worktree branch. No main merge, shared-binary replacement, remote publication or release was performed.","files":null}]}